    id: "note-61",
    title: "Music: Learning music theory as an adult",
    body: "Started formally learning music theory at 35. I've been playing guitar for 15 years, so I've absorbed a lot intuitively, but the formal vocabulary is different. Reading about voice leading, harmonic function, and cadences has actually made me a better improviser - not because I think about 'iv-V-I progressions' when playing, but because understanding the grammar of music helps you recognize patterns in what you hear and what you want to create. Most interesting: learning about non-functional harmony and how 20th century composers deliberately broke the rules that defined classical music. This has freed me from always trying to resolve tension in the 'correct' way - sometimes unresolved dissonance is more expressive.",
    createdAt: "2025-10-13",
